# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# reads/ — PENDING REMOVAL BEFORE THIS REPO GOES PUBLIC
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# reads/ holds working notes (REPORT.md, CLAUDE_PROJECT_ROADMAP.md,
# UNITY_PLAN.md) and the in-progress thesis article. It is NOT meant to be
# published.
#
# THIS ENTRY IS CURRENTLY INERT. .gitignore does not apply to files git
# already tracks, and reads/ is tracked in 18 of the repository's commits.
# Two separate things are therefore still outstanding:
#
#   1. stop tracking it:   git rm -r --cached reads
#   2. erase it from the existing history, because `git log -p -- reads/`
#      recovers every version of it otherwise. That needs a filter-repo
#      rewrite across all commits plus a force-push.
#
# Step 2 is the one that matters. Until it is done, MAKING THIS REPOSITORY
# PUBLIC PUBLISHES THE NOTES AND THE ARTICLE PDF, whatever the current tree
# looks like.
reads/

# The paper's working artefacts follow reads/, for the same reason: they are
# captures and intermediate renders made for a document that is not published
# from this repository. `article_screenings/` is the figure set captured for
# the paper; `outputs/paper/` is its build scratch, including page rasters of
# the PDF itself.
#
# The public showcase is NOT here. `docs/showcase/` holds a curated, resized
# subset, is tracked, and is what README.md links to. Rebuild it with
# `python docs/make_showcase.py` while the captures above are still on disk.
article_screenings/
outputs/paper/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# LaTeX build artifacts (reads/article.tex)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The .tex source and the built .pdf are both worth tracking; the
# intermediate files are not. (*.log is already ignored above.)
*.aux
*.toc
*.out
*.lof
*.lot
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk
*.synctex.gz
*.bbl
*.blg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTE ON `outputs/`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# outputs/*.png IS tracked on purpose (~3 MB). Those figures are the
# validation evidence REPORT.md cites section by section — the Haldane
# fit, the midparent-offspring regression, the mtDNA bottleneck vs its
# closed form. For a thesis the figure that backs a claim belongs in
# history next to the code that produced it, so a reviewer can check
# that they still match. Regenerate with:
#     python health_engine_prototype.py

# Exported world bundles for the Unity viewer are NOT tracked: they are
# regenerable output, not evidence, and frames.csv alone runs to tens of
# megabytes on a long run. Regenerate with:
#     python export_for_unity.py
outputs/unity/

# outputs/mpfb/ splits the same way. The two JSON files ARE tracked: they are
# the measured calibration of a third-party add-on, which is exactly the kind
# of number that has to be in history next to the code that read it. The FBX
# files and the downloaded MPFB source are not: they are megabytes of
# regenerable output. Regenerate everything with:
#     python run_mpfb_probe.py
outputs/mpfb/*.fbx
outputs/mpfb/_install/

# The CC0 asset pack download cache is NOT tracked, and this is a correction:
# 16 partial segments were committed in session 23 by accident and the
# completed pack is 267 MB on top of that. It is a byte-for-byte copy of a
# published third-party archive, it is resumable, and it re-downloads in under
# three minutes on sixteen connections, so nothing about it belongs in
# history. The CALIBRATION read out of it, health_engine/data/mpfb_assets.json,
# is tracked, because that is the measured number the code depends on.
# Regenerate with:
#     python run_mpfb_probe.py --install-assets
outputs/mpfb/assetpack/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unity
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `unity/com.samal.extnpc` IS tracked — it is source. A Unity *project*
# that consumes the package is not part of this repo; these patterns cover
# the case where one gets created here anyway.
#
# `run_unity_tests.py` generates exactly such a project, headlessly, so the
# package can be COMPILED and its Test Runner suite executed without a human
# opening the editor. The project is regenerable output — the script is the
# evidence, not the project — so neither it nor its logs are tracked.
unity/test-project/
unity/test-project-logs/
unity/**/Library/
unity/**/Temp/
unity/**/Logs/
unity/**/UserSettings/
unity/**/obj/
*.csproj
*.sln

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# uv and Hypothesis
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# uv.lock IS tracked, deliberately. It is the pin the reproducibility claim
# rests on: requirements.txt names the eight direct dependencies, the lock
# names all 49 with hashes. A lock nobody commits pins nothing.
#
# The environment it builds is not tracked, and neither is Hypothesis's
# example database, which is a local cache of inputs that have failed here.
.venv/
.hypothesis/
